01:44:58,000 --> 01:45:01,000
The stuff that they really like are the absolutely proven truths,

85
01:45:01,000 --> 01:45:05,000
and there is, I would say, a phobia of everything else.

86
01:45:05,000 --> 01:45:10,000
And like, so I think it's sort of a tendency to say, look, everything short of perfection is like all scum.

87
01:45:10,000 --> 01:45:16,000
It's all conjecture and opinion, and you know, so, who cares about that stuff?

88
01:45:16,000 --> 01:45:18,000
Okay.

89
01:45:18,000 --> 01:45:21,000
All right, so, questions, questions.

90
01:45:21,000 --> 01:45:23,000
All right.

91
01:45:23,000 --> 01:45:24,000
Okay.

92
01:45:24,000 --> 01:45:28,000
All right, so now we're going to go over two welfare theorems.

93
01:45:28,000 --> 01:45:34,000
And I wish we could be doing this earlier in the night when people's mathematical faculties are at their higher level,

94
01:45:34,000 --> 01:45:42,000
but, and I do actually plan my lectures to try to front load the math, but I can't always accomplish that, so, here we are.

95
01:45:42,000 --> 01:45:48,000
All right, so now you guys all remember going and proving that markets are coverage sufficient

96
01:45:48,000 --> 01:45:51,000
and you're just doing one supply and demand model, right?

97
01:45:51,000 --> 01:45:54,000
So, you may have even gone and taught this for a class.

98
01:45:54,000 --> 01:45:59,000
You've got Q, you've got P, you've got supply, or you've got demand, you've got supply,

99
01:45:59,000 --> 01:46:04,000
and then in this market equilibrium, we've maximized our coverage sufficiency, right?

100
01:46:04,000 --> 01:46:07,000
Everyone remember seeing a diagram like this?

101
01:46:07,000 --> 01:46:08,000
All right.

102
01:46:08,000 --> 01:46:14,000
Now, so, standard results with the intersection of supply and demand, total surplus maximized,

103
01:46:14,000 --> 01:46:18,000
allocation must be Pareto efficient, right?

104
01:46:18,000 --> 01:46:22,000
And remember I was talking last week about how in a highly stylized theoretical model,

105
01:46:22,000 --> 01:46:24,000
things might not be Pareto efficient?

106
01:46:24,000 --> 01:46:27,000
Well, how stylized does that look to you?

107
01:46:27,000 --> 01:46:28,000
Pretty stylized, all right?

108
01:46:28,000 --> 01:46:31,000
So, yes, so it might not be Pareto efficient.

109
01:46:31,000 --> 01:46:32,000
Okay.

110
01:46:32,000 --> 01:46:42,000
But can that diagram that we draw for undergraduates all the time actually be honestly generalized to multiple markets?

111
01:46:42,000 --> 01:46:45,000
Right? Is it okay to say, well, that's how it is for one market, all markets,

112
01:46:45,000 --> 01:46:49,000
so if all markets do that, then it's true for all markets moving on?

113
01:46:49,000 --> 01:46:51,000
Across markets?

114
01:46:51,000 --> 01:46:52,000
Yes.

115
01:46:52,000 --> 01:46:54,000
Equilibrium among markets?

116
01:46:54,000 --> 01:46:57,000
Well, no, no, no, basically you're saying, well, that's how one market works.

117
01:46:57,000 --> 01:46:59,000
So, this is how the corn market works.

118
01:46:59,000 --> 01:47:01,000
Yes, that's how, yeah, one market works.

119
01:47:01,000 --> 01:47:06,000
So, is it intellectually okay to draw that diagram and then generalize it to a world with 100 markets

120
01:47:06,000 --> 01:47:11,000
and say all the, like, the same thing, the same thing works no matter how many markets there are?

121
01:47:11,000 --> 01:47:14,000
So, that's a serious question.

122
01:47:14,000 --> 01:47:15,000
All right.

123
01:47:15,000 --> 01:47:18,000
And general equilibrium theory has an answer, which is yes, you can do that.

124
01:47:18,000 --> 01:47:19,000
It is okay.

125
01:47:19,000 --> 01:47:20,000
All right.

126
01:47:20,000 --> 01:47:27,000
So, you can generalize those easy textbook results for supply and demand to a complex general equilibrium model

127
01:47:27,000 --> 01:47:30,000
with any number of goods and actors.

128
01:47:30,000 --> 01:47:31,000
All right.

129
01:47:31,000 --> 01:47:35,000
So, there are actually two famous welfare theorems associated with this.

130
01:47:35,000 --> 01:47:38,000
The first one I'm actually going to walk you through the proof,

131
01:47:38,000 --> 01:47:41,000
and the second one I'm just going to tell you the answer.

132
01:47:41,000 --> 01:47:42,000
All right.

133
01:47:42,000 --> 01:47:46,000
So, the first welfare theorem says that under all the preceding assumptions that I've given you,

134
01:47:46,000 --> 01:47:51,000
the general equilibrium allocation is Brayton efficient, and actually, like, there's also Calgary,

135
01:47:51,000 --> 01:47:56,000
you know, also Calgary is efficient, but since it's a highly stylized theoretical setting,

136
01:47:56,000 --> 01:47:59,000
it's actually an interesting issue as to whether it's Brayton efficient.

137
01:47:59,000 --> 01:48:01,000
It's not just a trivial thing.

138
01:48:01,000 --> 01:48:02,000
All right.

139
01:48:02,000 --> 01:48:04,000
So, how do you prove this?

140
01:48:04,000 --> 01:48:05,000
All right.

141
01:48:05,000 --> 01:48:07,000
Well, there's a standard proof by contradiction.

142
01:48:07,000 --> 01:48:09,000
So, you've never heard of proof by contradiction?

143
01:48:09,000 --> 01:48:13,000
Now, the way proof by contradiction works is you want to prove A.

144
01:48:13,000 --> 01:48:19,000
So, what you do is say, well, let's just assume not A, and then let's work a reason through that,

145
01:48:19,000 --> 01:48:25,000
and then eventually, you know, if proof by contradiction works, assuming not A, you come to a contradiction,

146
01:48:25,000 --> 01:48:30,000
and if you reason correctly in all those steps, that must mean that not A was false.

147
01:48:30,000 --> 01:48:32,000
And if not A is false, what must be true?

148
01:48:32,000 --> 01:48:33,000
A.

149
01:48:33,000 --> 01:48:34,000
A. All right.

150
01:48:34,000 --> 01:48:36,000
So, that's how proof by contradiction works.

151
01:48:36,000 --> 01:48:37,000
All right.

152
01:48:37,000 --> 01:48:44,000
So, standard proof by contradiction, suppose that X is an equilibrium allocation, but X prime is Pareto superior to X.

153
01:48:44,000 --> 01:48:45,000
All right.

154
01:48:45,000 --> 01:48:46,000
So, these are the steps.

155
01:48:46,000 --> 01:48:47,000
All right.

156
01:48:47,000 --> 01:48:54,000
So, X prime must be feasible, so that means that the summation of all of the consumption vectors must still be less than

157
01:48:54,000 --> 01:49:01,000
or equal to the endowment vector, and remember, this is the summation of the new alternative consumption vectors, right,

158
01:49:01,000 --> 01:49:09,000
and your new, allegedly, Pareto superior equilibrium must actually still be feasible, and then price or non-negative.

159
01:49:09,000 --> 01:49:14,000
So, price or non-negative, when we go and multiply through the prices of all the consumption vectors,

160
01:49:14,000 --> 01:49:21,000
that must still be less than or equal to the prices times the endowment vectors, and again, remember, that,

161
01:49:21,000 --> 01:49:23,000
remember, what does that mean?

162
01:49:23,000 --> 01:49:28,000
This is just saying that the value of the stuff that you buy at the supermarket must be less than the value of the stuff

163
01:49:28,000 --> 01:49:34,000
that you sold at the supermarket, right, because it's a commodity economy, so the only place to get money is by

164
01:49:34,000 --> 01:49:39,000
selling stuff at the supermarket, right, and then we assume utility is strictly increasing consumption,

165
01:49:39,000 --> 01:49:47,000
so people always consume an amount equal to their total income, all right, and this implies that the market,

166
01:49:47,000 --> 01:49:53,000
the value of your new market basket must be less than or equal to the value of the original market basket.

167
01:49:53,000 --> 01:49:54,000
Okay.

168
01:49:54,000 --> 01:49:55,000
Great.

169
01:49:55,000 --> 01:50:02,000
But, by the definition of Pareto improvements, all consumers must weakly prefer X prime to X,

170
01:50:02,000 --> 01:50:05,000
and one must strictly prefer X prime to X.

171
01:50:05,000 --> 01:50:08,000
That's what it means for there to be a Pareto improvement.

172
01:50:08,000 --> 01:50:14,000
One person must actually like the new endowment better, and no one can like it less.

173
01:50:14,000 --> 01:50:15,000
Okay.

174
01:50:15,000 --> 01:50:20,000
So, weak preference means that X prime cannot be more affordable than X,

175
01:50:20,000 --> 01:50:25,000
so that means that PXI prime must be less than or equal to PX,

176
01:50:25,000 --> 01:50:30,000
and then strict preference requires that X prime be not affordable for at least one person,

177
01:50:30,000 --> 01:50:36,000
so for at least one person, the value of the new endowment must be greater than the value of,

178
01:50:36,000 --> 01:50:41,000
or excuse me, the value of the new consumption basket must be greater than the value of the old consumption basket, right,

179
01:50:41,000 --> 01:50:47,000
and now, if we sum these weak and strong inequalities, we get this result, which contradicts the original assumption.

180
01:50:47,000 --> 01:50:50,000
You're wondering, so what's going on here?

181
01:50:50,000 --> 01:50:53,000
The last step is the key step.

182
01:50:53,000 --> 01:51:03,000
So, suppose that X is exactly equal to 3 and Y is greater than 4.

183
01:51:03,000 --> 01:51:07,000
Alright, what can we say about X plus Y?

184
01:51:07,000 --> 01:51:11,000
Can we say that X plus Y is greater than or equal to 7?

185
01:51:11,000 --> 01:51:13,000
What can we say?

186
01:51:13,000 --> 01:51:16,000
It is strictly greater than 7, yes,

187
01:51:16,000 --> 01:51:20,000
because we're taking something that is equal to 3 and adding something on more than 4,

188
01:51:20,000 --> 01:51:23,000
so the sum of that must be more than 7.

189
01:51:23,000 --> 01:51:25,000
Okay, and that's what's going on in the last step,

190
01:51:25,000 --> 01:51:30,000
is we're adding up one thing that must exactly equal the regular thing,

191
01:51:30,000 --> 01:51:32,000
and another thing must be strictly greater than it,

192
01:51:32,000 --> 01:51:42,000
and that's how we actually wind up contradicting that original assumption for that the market value of,

193
01:51:42,000 --> 01:51:49,000
or let's see, the market value of the new consumption basket must be less than or equal to the market value of the old consumption basket.

194
01:51:49,000 --> 01:51:55,000
Because now we've got a result that the new consumption basket must have a market value greater than the old one,

195
01:51:55,000 --> 01:51:59,000
and it is a contradiction to say that it is less than or equal and that it is greater.

196
01:51:59,000 --> 01:52:01,000
So, there it is.

197
01:52:01,000 --> 01:52:04,000
Alright, so, again, if you want to work through it, you can,

198
01:52:04,000 --> 01:52:11,000
but this late hour probably isn't going to be that helpful to get all over in more great detail.

199
01:52:11,000 --> 01:52:14,000
So, this is the very famous first welfare theorem,

200
01:52:14,000 --> 01:52:17,000
which some people have actually looked at and said,

201
01:52:17,000 --> 01:52:19,000
see, this is a great vindication of markets,

202
01:52:19,000 --> 01:52:21,000
and other people, of course, have looked at it and said,

203
01:52:21,000 --> 01:52:23,000
no, if you understand what you need to make the theorem true,

204
01:52:23,000 --> 01:52:26,000
it is a great condemnation of markets.

205
01:52:26,000 --> 01:52:30,000
And then, after all the dust has settled, there's people saying,

206
01:52:30,000 --> 01:52:36,000
look, even if all these assumptions were true, it's not really that impressive,

207
01:52:36,000 --> 01:52:40,000
because an allocation where one person owns everything, is that proto-efficient?

208
01:52:40,000 --> 01:52:43,000
Sure. Alright.

209
01:52:43,000 --> 01:52:47,000
So, markets are proto-efficient.

210
01:52:47,000 --> 01:52:49,000
If you remember last week's lecture, it's even more impressive,

211
01:52:49,000 --> 01:52:53,000
because you have learned to believe that everything is proto-efficient, hopefully.

212
01:52:53,000 --> 01:52:57,000
So, then, wow, it's a theorem saying that markets are proto-efficient, just like everything.

213
01:52:57,000 --> 01:52:59,000
Wow, that's really great.

214
01:52:59,000 --> 01:53:02,000
It is not as dumb as that, because remember,

215
01:53:02,000 --> 01:53:06,000
this is actually a result of a highly stylized theoretical setting,

216
01:53:06,000 --> 01:53:08,000
where we could actually get a different result.

217
01:53:08,000 --> 01:53:10,000
And actually, you can go and prove things, like,

218
01:53:10,000 --> 01:53:14,000
if there are externalities, then the result would generally not be proto-efficient.

219
01:53:14,000 --> 01:53:17,000
You're saying, like, well, why would it not be proto-efficient?

220
01:53:17,000 --> 01:53:21,000
How in the real world could you go and solve an externality without hurting somebody?

221
01:53:21,000 --> 01:53:23,000
In the real world, you can't, right?

222
01:53:23,000 --> 01:53:25,000
Because, like, suppose everyone is polluting,

223
01:53:25,000 --> 01:53:28,000
and you go and impose a pollution tax, and that cleans up the air,

224
01:53:28,000 --> 01:53:30,000
and people's health is better.

225
01:53:30,000 --> 01:53:34,000
Still, who is almost certainly going to be harmed by that pollution tax?

226
01:53:34,000 --> 01:53:38,000
Yeah, polluters, or how about someone who pollutes a lot,

227
01:53:38,000 --> 01:53:42,000
doesn't like going outside, and is on the verge of death anyway, right?

228
01:53:42,000 --> 01:53:46,000
Or, like, they already have emphysema, so they don't care what the air quality is like.

229
01:53:46,000 --> 01:53:49,000
All right, so, in the real world, you're like,

230
01:53:49,000 --> 01:53:51,000
there's going to be someone who doesn't gain, right?

231
01:53:51,000 --> 01:53:55,000
But on a homework problem, you could easily come up with a situation where everybody gained.

232
01:53:55,000 --> 01:53:59,000
Right, so, let's suppose a homework problem where

233
01:53:59,000 --> 01:54:02,000
every single person places the same value,

234
01:54:02,000 --> 01:54:05,000
owns equal amounts of polluting,

235
01:54:05,000 --> 01:54:07,000
every single person has the same health function,

236
01:54:07,000 --> 01:54:09,000
everyone cares about the health to some degree,

237
01:54:09,000 --> 01:54:12,000
there's continuity, and that setting, then,

238
01:54:12,000 --> 01:54:16,000
is going to be really easy, actually, to say we go and impose a modest pollution tax,

239
01:54:16,000 --> 01:54:20,000
and then we go and we redistribute the tax in lump sum,

240
01:54:20,000 --> 01:54:22,000
and then everyone will be better off.

241
01:54:22,000 --> 01:54:25,000
So, that's a case where if we go and make the world,

242
01:54:25,000 --> 01:54:27,000
and we create a weird enough hypothetical,

243
01:54:27,000 --> 01:54:30,000
and again, we reverse engineer the hypothetical to make it work,

244
01:54:30,000 --> 01:54:32,000
then it's fine.

245
01:54:32,000 --> 01:54:34,000
Because remember, I didn't offer you any kind of a theorem

246
01:54:34,000 --> 01:54:36,000
that the world is always Pareto efficient last week.

247
01:54:36,000 --> 01:54:39,000
I offered you common sense observations about

248
01:54:39,000 --> 01:54:40,000
how it's really hard to compensate everybody,

249
01:54:40,000 --> 01:54:42,000
really hard to know how much to compensate people,

250
01:54:42,000 --> 01:54:45,000
and how there's really weird and sick and twisted people in the world.

251
01:54:45,000 --> 01:54:48,000
Which are all true, but that's not a theorem.

252
01:54:48,000 --> 01:54:50,000
It's just saying, look at the world with your eyes, people,

253
01:54:50,000 --> 01:54:52,000
and see what the world is really like,

254
01:54:52,000 --> 01:54:56,000
and realize you're not going to be able to get a Pareto improvement in the real world.

255
01:54:56,000 --> 01:54:57,000
All right.

256
01:54:57,000 --> 01:55:01,000
In any case, the fact that you can show that

257
01:55:01,000 --> 01:55:05,000
an economy with any number of markets that satisfies these assumptions is Pareto efficient

258
01:55:05,000 --> 01:55:07,000
is not completely trivial,

259
01:55:07,000 --> 01:55:12,000
because the problem is set up to be so clean

260
01:55:12,000 --> 01:55:15,000
that actually it might not be Pareto efficient.

261
01:55:15,000 --> 01:55:16,000
Did you have a question over there?

262
01:55:16,000 --> 01:55:20,000
I was just going to ask if anyone's ever made an effort

