01:68:20,320 --> 01:68:21,320
Reversal of reversal.

210
01:68:26,320 --> 01:68:28,320
All right.

211
01:68:28,320 --> 01:68:30,120
So the second law of the theorem,

212
01:68:30,120 --> 01:68:34,200
very important if it is a debate between the liberal

213
01:68:34,200 --> 01:68:36,840
comments of the 1950s and the Marxist economists.

214
01:68:36,840 --> 01:68:38,480
Of course, if you're someone that is just

215
01:68:38,480 --> 01:68:41,160
sympathetic to market distribution of income anyway,

216
01:68:41,160 --> 01:68:44,000
then you may say, well, I don't see what the big deal is.

217
01:68:44,000 --> 01:68:46,280
Or if you're also someone who knows anything

218
01:68:46,280 --> 01:68:48,040
about the history of inequality and says,

219
01:68:48,040 --> 01:68:51,280
look, the main inequalities are caused by governments going

220
01:68:51,280 --> 01:68:53,920
and looting and enslaving the rest of the population.

221
01:68:53,920 --> 01:68:56,120
Get rid of that, and the objectionable inequality is

222
01:68:56,120 --> 01:68:57,080
gone.

223
01:68:57,080 --> 01:69:00,240
Or if you are familiar with the current global distribution

224
01:69:00,240 --> 01:69:03,000
income, the single main determinant

225
01:69:03,000 --> 01:69:04,400
of your income in the modern world

226
01:69:04,400 --> 01:69:08,280
is which country you happen to be born in.

227
01:69:08,280 --> 01:69:09,880
And why is that?

228
01:69:09,880 --> 01:69:12,320
Because of immigration restrictions, of course.

229
01:69:12,320 --> 01:69:14,880
Because all you really need to do in this world,

230
01:69:14,880 --> 01:69:17,240
if you are an able-bodied adult, is

231
01:69:17,240 --> 01:69:19,400
to go and get steers passes to rich country

232
01:69:19,400 --> 01:69:20,600
and get a job shedding shoes.

233
01:69:20,600 --> 01:69:23,640
And you are no longer anywhere near absolute poverty

234
01:69:23,640 --> 01:69:25,920
as it's globally defined.

235
01:69:25,920 --> 01:69:26,960
Why doesn't this happen?

236
01:69:26,960 --> 01:69:28,400
Because it's illegal.

237
01:69:28,400 --> 01:69:30,560
How can this be with all the illegal immigration?

238
01:69:30,560 --> 01:69:31,960
The amount of illegal immigration

239
01:69:31,960 --> 01:69:33,680
is a pittance compared to the amount that

240
01:69:33,680 --> 01:69:35,560
would happen if it were legal.

241
01:69:35,560 --> 01:69:39,600
So if you say there's like 11 million illegal immigrants

242
01:69:39,600 --> 01:69:42,600
currently in the US, so anyone can come,

243
01:69:42,600 --> 01:69:45,840
how many do you think would be here if anybody could come?

244
01:69:45,840 --> 01:69:47,800
Well, there's a lot of estimates,

245
01:69:47,800 --> 01:69:50,040
but generally in the hundreds of millions,

246
01:69:50,040 --> 01:69:52,760
saying that the laws that exist, whatever you think about them,

247
01:69:52,760 --> 01:69:58,800
are way over 90% effective in preventing immigration.

248
01:69:58,800 --> 01:70:01,240
OK, so questions, questions.

249
01:70:01,240 --> 01:70:04,280
All right, so that's the first and second level of theorems.

250
01:70:04,280 --> 01:70:10,040
All right, now moving on to something more fun stuff.

251
01:70:10,040 --> 01:70:13,160
All right, so Aaron DeBrow contingent claims markets.

252
01:70:13,160 --> 01:70:15,840
So general equilibrium seems pretty general

253
01:70:15,840 --> 01:70:17,880
because you've got a model that fits

254
01:70:17,880 --> 01:70:21,360
the economy with any number of people, any number of goods.

255
01:70:21,360 --> 01:70:23,880
So that sounds like a big deal.

256
01:70:23,880 --> 01:70:26,380
But Aaron DeBrow noticed that it's actually way more general

257
01:70:26,380 --> 01:70:28,800
than it seems on the surface because general equilibrium

258
01:70:28,800 --> 01:70:31,200
can handle intertemporal exchange.

259
01:70:31,200 --> 01:70:34,560
It can handle a trade of dollars today for dollars

260
01:70:34,560 --> 01:70:36,800
and 20 years ago, for bananas today,

261
01:70:36,800 --> 01:70:38,800
for squashes in three minutes.

262
01:70:38,800 --> 01:70:41,280
All of these intertemporal trades can be handled.

263
01:70:41,280 --> 01:70:43,800
All you need to do is take the model

264
01:70:43,800 --> 01:70:48,120
and then say let's define good k at time t

265
01:70:48,120 --> 01:70:52,200
as being a different good than good k at time t plus 1.

266
01:70:52,200 --> 01:70:55,680
So let's just go and multiply all the physical goods

267
01:70:55,680 --> 01:70:58,520
by all the possible times in which those goods could exist

268
01:70:58,520 --> 01:71:00,960
and then rescale the model so that no longer

269
01:71:00,960 --> 01:71:01,960
is the number of goods k.

270
01:71:01,960 --> 01:71:06,160
It's equal to k times the number of possible time periods.

271
01:71:06,160 --> 01:71:07,920
So in other words, instead of trading current goods,

272
01:71:07,920 --> 01:71:09,780
you'd be trading slips of paper with promises

273
01:71:09,780 --> 01:71:12,640
to deliver any good at any time period.

274
01:71:12,640 --> 01:71:16,200
And now we've got intertemporal markets covered.

275
01:71:16,200 --> 01:71:19,680
So I could go and see actually I wrote these earlier.

276
01:71:19,680 --> 01:71:21,480
So I could give you a piece of paper

277
01:71:21,480 --> 01:71:24,640
that is good for a bundle of bananas in 2018

278
01:71:24,640 --> 01:71:26,440
and I could sell this to you.

279
01:71:26,440 --> 01:71:29,760
Now obviously, I can't sell you 2018 bananas right now

280
01:71:29,760 --> 01:71:30,640
in a physical sense.

281
01:71:30,640 --> 01:71:33,160
I can't go and say here are some 2018 bananas.

282
01:71:33,160 --> 01:71:35,800
If I handed you some bananas, right now

283
01:71:35,800 --> 01:71:37,320
they wouldn't be 2018 bananas.

284
01:71:37,320 --> 01:71:40,400
They'd be 2017 bananas.

285
01:71:40,400 --> 01:71:43,600
But I could give you a piece of paper, which is good for a bushel

286
01:71:43,600 --> 01:71:45,280
of bananas in 2018.

287
01:71:45,280 --> 01:71:46,860
And there's a certain amount of money

288
01:71:46,860 --> 01:71:48,440
if you're willing to pay me for that.

289
01:71:51,400 --> 01:71:53,200
Furthermore, general equilibrium can

290
01:71:53,200 --> 01:71:55,360
handle an arbitrary amount of uncertainty.

291
01:71:55,360 --> 01:71:57,560
So think about good k if x happens

292
01:71:57,560 --> 01:72:02,720
as being a different good than if not x happens.

293
01:72:02,720 --> 01:72:06,080
And actually, we can go and list every possible event,

294
01:72:06,080 --> 01:72:09,000
every possible good, multiply those two things together,

295
01:72:09,000 --> 01:72:12,080
and then we have a new number of goods.

296
01:72:12,080 --> 01:72:14,600
And we could do it number of physical goods

297
01:72:14,600 --> 01:72:17,200
times number of time periods times number

298
01:72:17,200 --> 01:72:18,720
of states of the world.

299
01:72:18,720 --> 01:72:21,800
And then that could come up to some enormous number

300
01:72:21,800 --> 01:72:24,280
when you multiply those three variables together and say

301
01:72:24,280 --> 01:72:25,840
that's the number of goods.

302
01:72:25,840 --> 01:72:28,880
And then plug that into Arrow to Brow.

303
01:72:28,880 --> 01:72:31,720
So I can have an endowment of bananas in 2018

304
01:72:31,720 --> 01:72:34,080
if average temperatures exceed 80 degrees.

305
01:72:34,080 --> 01:72:35,880
And then I can have another piece of paper

306
01:72:35,880 --> 01:72:38,120
that's good for those bananas if average temperatures

307
01:72:38,120 --> 01:72:40,120
are less than 80 degrees.

308
01:72:40,120 --> 01:72:42,240
And then we could go and sell all these little slips

309
01:72:42,240 --> 01:72:45,200
of paper, good under certain conditions,

310
01:72:45,200 --> 01:72:48,800
otherwise non-joy.

311
01:72:48,800 --> 01:72:52,720
When you have a coupon saying only good on these dates,

312
01:72:52,720 --> 01:72:55,640
only good with the purchase of a hamburger,

313
01:72:55,640 --> 01:72:57,800
so you've seen coupons like that.

314
01:72:57,800 --> 01:73:00,000
So again, you can imagine going and auctioning off

315
01:73:00,000 --> 01:73:02,440
all kinds of slips of paper, good for different commodities

316
01:73:02,440 --> 01:73:07,480
at different times under different circumstances.

317
01:73:07,480 --> 01:73:09,840
And in fact, if a piece of paper represents

318
01:73:09,840 --> 01:73:11,560
an unconditional good, then I could

319
01:73:11,560 --> 01:73:14,080
go and separate the piece of paper

320
01:73:14,080 --> 01:73:16,320
into 1,000 different regions, cut them all up

321
01:73:16,320 --> 01:73:17,820
with a pair of scissors, and then I

322
01:73:17,820 --> 01:73:20,240
could sell each one for, of course,

323
01:73:20,240 --> 01:73:25,680
much less than the value of the good under all conditions.

324
01:73:25,680 --> 01:73:28,200
So anyway, the idea is that in both cases,

325
01:73:28,200 --> 01:73:30,560
the complex problem is isomorphic

326
01:73:30,560 --> 01:73:31,760
to the standard problem.

327
01:73:31,760 --> 01:73:33,880
It's mathematically identical.

328
01:73:33,880 --> 01:73:35,680
So on the surface, it looks different.

329
01:73:35,680 --> 01:73:37,120
But when you really think about it,

330
01:73:37,120 --> 01:73:38,520
there's no fundamental difference

331
01:73:38,520 --> 01:73:42,320
between the complex problem and the simple problem.

332
01:73:42,320 --> 01:73:44,480
And therefore, all the standard results go through.

333
01:73:44,480 --> 01:73:47,280
All right, now, I would say that none of this

334
01:73:47,280 --> 01:73:50,720
would impress me if it weren't for an incredible application

335
01:73:50,720 --> 01:73:55,560
of this idea, which is the idea of the betting market.

336
01:73:59,360 --> 01:74:01,120
Right here at George Mason, we have

337
01:74:01,120 --> 01:74:06,280
one of the world's most prominent scholars

338
01:74:06,280 --> 01:74:07,080
on betting markets.

339
01:74:07,080 --> 01:74:08,360
That's Robin Hanson.

340
01:74:08,360 --> 01:74:10,520
So he really was one of the earliest people

341
01:74:10,520 --> 01:74:12,320
to talk about the ideas of betting markets.

342
01:74:12,320 --> 01:74:14,400
He's been involved in some of the key experiments

343
01:74:14,400 --> 01:74:16,120
with betting markets.

344
01:74:16,120 --> 01:74:20,760
The idea of a betting market is that I could actually

345
01:74:20,760 --> 01:74:23,960
go and say, look, here is a asset which

346
01:74:23,960 --> 01:74:28,880
will pay $1 if Donald Trump wins re-election.

347
01:74:28,880 --> 01:74:32,000
And then we can go and sell it.

348
01:74:32,000 --> 01:74:34,880
And we can look at that market price.

349
01:74:34,880 --> 01:74:38,640
And we can then go and use that market price

350
01:74:38,640 --> 01:74:40,520
to estimate what is the actual market

351
01:74:40,520 --> 01:74:42,880
estimate of the probability of this event happening.

352
01:74:42,880 --> 01:74:44,680
And we can do this for anything.

353
01:74:44,680 --> 01:74:48,240
We could go and have all kinds of submarkets.

354
01:74:48,240 --> 01:74:52,440
There is, so what is it?

355
01:74:52,440 --> 01:74:55,560
There's the price of this asset.

356
01:74:55,560 --> 01:74:58,000
So it says this pays $1 if Donald Trump wins,

357
01:74:58,000 --> 01:75:02,200
if he goes and kills more than 1,000 people in wars.

358
01:75:02,200 --> 01:75:03,320
And there's another asset.

359
01:75:03,320 --> 01:75:06,880
This is the price of $1 if he gets re-elected,

360
01:75:06,880 --> 01:75:09,760
if he kills less than or equal to 1,000 people.

361
01:75:09,760 --> 01:75:12,560
And we could have both these assets circulating.

362
01:75:12,560 --> 01:75:14,640
We could have thousands of assets circulating.

363
01:75:14,640 --> 01:75:18,360
All with very precise conditions of a payoff.

364
01:75:18,360 --> 01:75:21,200
And we can go and do a little bit of math

365
01:75:21,200 --> 01:75:24,840
to use all this to get a market estimate of what people

366
01:75:24,840 --> 01:75:27,120
actually think.

367
01:75:27,120 --> 01:75:28,880
Now, of course, the fact that you get a market estimate

368
01:75:28,880 --> 01:75:30,920
doesn't mean the market estimate is good.

369
01:75:30,920 --> 01:75:33,760
Could be the markets are run by idiots.

370
01:75:33,760 --> 01:75:36,800
But if you talk to Robin or read some of his research,

371
01:75:36,800 --> 01:75:39,520
there is a big literature to try to see

372
01:75:39,520 --> 01:75:42,280
how good are betting markets as actual predictors

373
01:75:42,280 --> 01:75:44,320
of future events.

374
01:75:44,320 --> 01:75:47,160
And the answer is, I guess there's two answers.

375
01:75:47,160 --> 01:75:49,280
One is, they're not that great.

376
01:75:49,280 --> 01:75:52,040
Second of all, they're better than anything else.

377
01:75:52,040 --> 01:75:56,880
So they are the least bad method of predicting future events.

378
01:75:56,880 --> 01:75:58,480
And how good are they?

379
01:75:58,480 --> 01:76:00,400
There's all kinds of weird results out there.

380
01:76:00,400 --> 01:76:03,800
Like, so betting markets for, well actually,

381
01:76:03,800 --> 01:76:06,000
so this is actually, like, commodities markets

382
01:76:06,000 --> 01:76:09,320
for oranges, which are essentially a betting

383
01:76:09,320 --> 01:76:11,720
market on the price of oranges.

384
01:76:11,720 --> 01:76:13,800
These predict the weather in Florida

385
01:76:13,800 --> 01:76:17,720
above and beyond all meteorological forecasts.

386
01:76:17,720 --> 01:76:19,560
So you could know everything meteorologists

387
01:76:19,560 --> 01:76:21,440
have to say about the weather in Florida.

388
01:76:21,440 --> 01:76:23,760
And then you go and you get a prediction of the weather.

389
01:76:23,760 --> 01:76:26,680
And then you go and you look at the market price.

390
01:76:26,680 --> 01:76:28,080
And of course, with good weather,

391
01:76:28,080 --> 01:76:29,560
you expect there to be low prices of oranges.

392
01:76:29,560 --> 01:76:30,960
With bad prices, you expect there

393
01:76:30,960 --> 01:76:32,600
to be high prices of oranges in Florida.

394
01:76:32,600 --> 01:76:34,600
Bad Florida weather, that is.

395
01:76:34,600 --> 01:76:38,040
And it gives you additional predictive value there.

396
01:76:38,040 --> 01:76:40,320
So looking at that commodity market,

397
01:76:40,320 --> 01:76:44,640
that tells you more than all the meteorologists know.

398
01:76:44,640 --> 01:76:46,480
Weird.

399
01:76:46,480 --> 01:76:49,640
And many other experiments along these lines,

400
01:76:49,640 --> 01:76:53,120
all coming down to if you are limited to information

401
01:76:53,120 --> 01:76:56,240
that you actually possess, that human beings have,

402
01:76:56,240 --> 01:76:58,200
and you want to go and make a prediction,

403
01:76:58,200 --> 01:77:00,000
you can only have one piece of information,

404
01:77:00,000 --> 01:77:04,120
betting markets are the best thing you can get.

405
01:77:04,120 --> 01:77:07,880
Now, broadband is very famously associated with not just

406
01:77:07,880 --> 01:77:13,440
betting markets, but the terrorism betting market.

407
01:77:13,440 --> 01:77:15,120
Right, yes, there was.

408
01:77:15,120 --> 01:77:17,800
It was not, of course, called the terrorism betting market.

409
01:77:17,800 --> 01:77:21,920
And the betting market was not only about terrorism.

410
01:77:21,920 --> 01:77:24,160
But it was about terrorism.

411
01:77:24,160 --> 01:77:27,280
Now, if you talk to Robin, he will dispute that last claim

412
01:77:27,280 --> 01:77:28,600
and with the following argument.

413
01:77:28,600 --> 01:77:31,000
He said, look, a terrorism betting market

414
01:77:31,000 --> 01:77:34,360
would mean that we were betting on specific terrorist attacks.

415
01:77:34,360 --> 01:77:35,480
But we weren't doing that.

416
01:77:35,480 --> 01:77:37,720
We were only betting on things like overall terrorist

417
01:77:37,720 --> 01:77:39,720
fatalities in a year.

418
01:77:39,720 --> 01:77:43,280
That's not a terrorism betting market, is it?

419
01:77:43,280 --> 01:77:46,120
This is the case where I tell Robin, you are a genius,

420
01:77:46,120 --> 01:77:49,800
but you don't understand any normal human being.

421
01:77:49,800 --> 01:77:52,040
To any normal human being, of course, your distinction

422
01:77:52,040 --> 01:77:55,320
makes no difference at all if human lives are being bet on,

423
01:77:55,320 --> 01:77:57,720
and whether it's a specific event or an aggregate

424
01:77:57,720 --> 01:77:59,000
or whatever, it doesn't matter.

425
01:77:59,000 --> 01:78:00,420
That's a terrorism betting market,

426
01:78:00,420 --> 01:78:01,580
and it's offensive to them.

427
01:78:04,720 --> 01:78:06,040
Sometimes Robin goes, OK.

428
01:78:06,040 --> 01:78:07,840
And sometimes he's like, I don't see that.

429
01:78:07,840 --> 01:78:10,000
Well, I don't know.

430
01:78:10,000 --> 01:78:11,840
Do you have to go and find some normal people

431
01:78:11,840 --> 01:78:13,580
and talk to them to see what they'll say?

432
01:78:13,580 --> 01:78:15,600
Because they will say this.

433
01:78:15,600 --> 01:78:16,560
You can bet on it.

434
01:78:19,360 --> 01:78:23,740
But anyway, so he was part of some betting markets

435
01:78:23,740 --> 01:78:25,760
where you bet on the total number of fatalities

436
01:78:25,760 --> 01:78:26,840
from terrorism in a year.

437
01:78:29,400 --> 01:78:32,720
Now, what's the point of this supposed to be?

438
01:78:32,720 --> 01:78:34,320
Well, first of all, to get an estimate

439
01:78:34,320 --> 01:78:37,440
of how bad terrorism is going to be,

440
01:78:37,440 --> 01:78:40,080
and then furthermore, as I was suggesting,

441
01:78:40,080 --> 01:78:44,280
you can go and create submarkets to try to find out,

442
01:78:44,280 --> 01:78:47,400
is a government policy going to make terrorism better or worse?

443
01:78:47,400 --> 01:78:49,040
So you could say terrorism fatalities,

444
01:78:49,040 --> 01:78:51,480
if we invade Iraq in 2003, terrorism fatalities

445
01:78:51,480 --> 01:78:54,480
if we don't invade Iraq in 2003, and do a little bit of math,

446
01:78:54,480 --> 01:78:56,960
and then you could actually come up with a market estimate

447
01:78:56,960 --> 01:78:59,240
about whether a given course of action

448
01:78:59,240 --> 01:79:01,560
is actually going to be an effective way of dealing

449
01:79:01,560 --> 01:79:04,320
with the problem or counterproductive or have no

450
01:79:04,320 --> 01:79:04,820
effect.

451
01:79:04,820 --> 01:79:05,320
I get it.

452
01:79:05,320 --> 01:79:08,320
It's like if you were actually a terrorist,

453
01:79:08,320 --> 01:79:10,600
it would make a lot of sense to get on this market.

454
01:79:10,600 --> 01:79:11,320
Absolutely.

455
01:79:11,320 --> 01:79:14,000
So that was one of the main reasons

456
01:79:14,000 --> 01:79:17,800
why this was shut down in two days.

457
01:79:17,800 --> 01:79:20,840
However, as you might guess, there

458
01:79:20,840 --> 01:79:22,680
were very smart people involved who

459
01:79:22,680 --> 01:79:24,840
thought of that and 1,000 other objections,

460
01:79:24,840 --> 01:79:28,000
and they worked it all into the market in order to deal with it.

461
01:79:28,000 --> 01:79:30,640
There were many things they did in order to avoid this problem.

462
01:79:30,640 --> 01:79:33,180
First of all, to participate in the terrorism betting market,

463
01:79:33,180 --> 01:79:35,600
you had to be registered with the CIA under your real name,

464
01:79:35,600 --> 01:79:37,960
or you could never collect any money.

465
01:79:37,960 --> 01:79:38,480
All right.

466
01:79:38,480 --> 01:79:41,440
So what this meant is if you bet on terrorism

467
01:79:41,440 --> 01:79:44,000
and you made a ton of money, while you would be paid,

468
01:79:44,000 --> 01:79:46,120
there would be a very thorough investigation of you

469
01:79:46,120 --> 01:79:47,480
to see whether you would possibly

470
01:79:47,480 --> 01:79:49,840
have anything to do with it.

471
